{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Upon the break of dawn, thou shalt arise and make thy way to the airport, where thou shalt board a flight bound for the land of the rising sun, Japan.\n\nUpon arrival in Tokyo, the capital city, thou shalt repair to thy lodgings and rest from thy journey.\n\nThe following day, thou shalt venture forth to explore the wonders of Tokyo. Visit the Meiji Shrine, a Shinto shrine dedicated to the deified spirits of Emperor Meiji and his wife, Empress Shōken. Then, stroll through the Yoyogi Park, a respite of green in the midst of the city.\n\nIn the afternoon, make thy way to the Asakusa district, where thou shalt find the Sensō-ji, a Buddhist temple dedicated to the goddess of mercy, Kannon. Peruse the Nakamise Shopping Street, which leads to the temple, for souvenirs and snacks.\n\nOn the third day, travel to the ancient city of Kamakura, once the political center of Japan. Here, thou shalt find the Great Buddha of Kamakura, a massive bronze statue of Amida Buddha, as well as numerous other temples and shrines.\n\nOn the fourth day, venture to the city of Hakone, famous for its hot springs, natural beauty, and the view of nearby Mt. Fuji. Take a boat ride on Lake Ashi and enjoy the scenic views.\n\nOn the fifth day, return to Tokyo and spend the day as thou wilt, perhaps visiting some of the city's many museums or indulging in some retail therapy.\n\nOn the sixth day, travel to Kyoto, the cultural heart of Japan. Here, thou shalt find countless temples, shrines, and gardens, including the Kinkaku-ji, a Zen temple whose top two floors are completely covered in gold leaf.\n\nOn the seventh day, explore the Arashiyama Bamboo Grove, a natural forest of bamboo in western Kyoto. Then, visit the Fushimi Inari Shrine, known for its thousands of vermilion torii gates, which form a tunnel up the mountain.\n\nOn the eighth day, return to Tokyo and prepare 